  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nShowers have come to an end this morning with skies quickly \nclearing. Mid-level water vapor satellite imagery shows the low \nthat passed through California this morning is already over \nNevada, with drier air filtering in on the back side of the low. \nZonal flow today transitions to weak ridging by Thursday, allowing\nfor one final morning of marine layer clouds along the coastline \nThursday morning. Still, drier air will begin to mix in as \noffshore flow returns, and any clouds on Thursday will likely \nclear quickly after sunrise. Temperatures warm tomorrow by around \n5 degrees over today for areas west of the mountains, staying \nsteady for eastern areas as the weak ridge sets up. By Friday, \noffshore flow intensifies with a more robust high as global models\nhone in on a weak to moderate Santa Ana event for Friday through \nSunday. With these northeasterly winds, warmer temperatures and \ndrier conditions are in store of the weekend and highs for areas \nwest of the mountains soar to the mid to upper 80s. Minor HeatRisk\nreturns for the western regions as early as Friday, with \nlocalized areas of moderate HeatRisk for Saturday and Sunday. \nHumidity will fall to the teens this weekend for valleys and \ncoastal mountains, with poor overnight recovery 35-50%. \n\nTemperatures remain abnormally warm on Monday with high pressure \nover the region, despite a decrease in the offshore flow. Things \ncooldown a bit by Tuesday as the high shifts eastward and onshore \nflow resumes. The pattern mid to late next week remains chaotic, \nwith both the GFS and ECMWF indicating a return to cooler weather\nand precipitation, but with very different outcomes. At this \npoint confidence is high for a pattern shift, but as to what that \nwill mean for SoCal remains to be seen.",
